#!/usr/bin/env bash
# =====================================================================
# THE NATURAL MACHINE LOOP — complete hands-free execution.
#
#   ./run_the_natural_machine_forever              one cycle, now
#   ./run_the_natural_machine_forever --daemon     loop, while the process lives
#   ./run_the_natural_machine_forever --supervise  loop, respawning its worker
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# CORRECTION, 2026-08-14, before anyone relies on the previous sentence.
#
# This header used to say "No human input. No agent required. Start it
# and walk away."  ~~That was true.~~  It was NOT.  I started the daemon,
# watched it complete cycle 0, reported it running, and it was DEAD
# within minutes — as was `./sync`.  In this container a backgrounded
# process does not outlive the shell session that spawned it, `setsid`
# included.  I asserted persistence on the evidence that it STARTED.
# That is the same error as reading a warning for an error or a missing
# name for a green: a claim about a state, evidenced by an event.
#
# The correction is not incidental.  A sibling swarm verified the same
# defect independently the same hour — "the math machine was DEAD, ~1h
# before anyone noticed" (msg 0463) — and the reason nobody noticed is
# that a dead loop and a quiet loop look identical from the repository.
#
# So the loop no longer relies on staying alive.  It now
#
#   * stamps a DUE-BY time every cycle, into the file people read, so
#     that death is self-reporting to anyone who opens it and needs no
#     process to detect;
#   * is idempotent and cheap to re-enter, so any arriving mind runs one
#     cycle and the ledger advances;
#   * offers --supervise for environments where processes do persist.
#
# Hands-free does not mean a process that never dies.  It means nothing
# breaks when it does, and the next arrival can see that it did.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT IT IS, AND WHY IT IS NOT CI
#
# A build server treats a red check as an error to be fixed.  This loop
# treats it as an object to be computed, on the authority of Delta 15
# T15.81 (`NaturalMachine.StructuredDefect`, checked):
#
#     a representation map either transports everything, or at least one
#     of its homotopy fibers is noncontractible or empty.
#
# C15.82: *every failed equivalence contains a precise reconstruction
# question in its fibers.*  So there is no "failure" outcome here.  A
# module that does not check is a FIBER, recorded as one, and the loop
# keeps running.  That is the whole difference, and it is why this can be
# left unattended: nothing it meets is an exception.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE FOUR PHASES, each answering to a checked theorem
#
#   1. SYNC       publish and absorb.  Owner rule 2026-08-14.
#   2. GATE       check every module SEPARATELY and record its own exit
#                 code.  Never the aggregate alone.
#   3. LEDGER     append the cycle: greens, fibers, and the walk frontier.
#   4. DRAW       cut a fresh uncurated door so whatever attaches next
#                 does not enter through the conspicuous documents.
#
# Phase 2 is per-module because this repository has produced the same
# defect twice in one day in opposite directions:  a WARNING read as an
# error (`UnsupportedIndexedMatch`, correction 0395), and a MISSING NAME
# read as a green for a day (`injectSuc`, msg 0456), plus a BUILD.md
# claiming "every module, exit 0" while checking a minority of them.
# All three survive any check that looks at aggregate output instead of
# `$?` per module.
#
#     A green is an exit code, and only for what was actually run.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# LEDGER ROW SEMANTICS — exact, 2026-08-16 (state-vs-event discipline)
#
# One row = one EVENT: "this container, carrying this toolchain, invoked
# these checks at this time and observed these exit codes."  A row is
# never a STATE of the mathematics.  Reading it as one is the same error
# corrected at the top of this header — a claim about a state, evidenced
# by an event — and it happened in this very ledger: the
# 2026-08-14T17:19Z row read "315 modules, 0 green, 315 fibers" as if
# the corpus had died in twelve hours.  What actually happened is two
# superimposed events that the old schema could not distinguish:
#   (a) the tree genuinely grew 87 -> 315 modules that day (sibling
#       swarm landings; verified from git, not from this ledger), and
#   (b) the cycle ran in a fresh container with NO `agda` ON PATH, so
#       every per-module invocation exited 127 (command not found) —
#       the aggregate's 127 is the tell; Agda itself, when present,
#       exits 0 or 1, never 127.
# Zero mathematics was checked and zero mathematics was broken.  The
# row recorded the container and called it the corpus.
#
# Columns (tab-separated, header row first, append-only after that):
#   utc            when the row was appended (event time, UTC).
#   cycle          ordinal = number of data rows before this one.  A
#                  count of ledger events, not a clock: a gap in `utc`
#                  is silence (no machine ran), never "missed cycles".
#   toolchain      the checker that produced this row's exit codes,
#                  e.g. `agda-2.6.3`, or `absent` when no agda was on
#                  PATH.  The tree's pin is Agda 2.8.0 + cubical v0.9
#                  (formal/cubical/BUILD.md "Toolchain"); a row whose
#                  toolchain differs from the pin carries version-skew
#                  fibers that are evidence about the SKEW, not the
#                  tree, and BUILD.md "Where the skew bites" must be
#                  read next to it.
#   modules        how many modules were actually invoked this cycle.
#                  0 when toolchain=absent: a check that cannot start
#                  is 0 checks performed, not N failed ones.
#   green          count of exit 0.  An exit code, only for what was
#                  actually run — never parsed output, never inferred.
#   fiber          count of nonzero exits that are Agda's own verdict
#                  (exit < 124: the process ran and refused the
#                  module).  These are the C15.82 fibers — the only
#                  column that ever assigns mathematical work.
#   fiber_env      count of nonzero exits that are the environment's,
#                  not Agda's (exit >= 124: 124 watchdog timeout,
#                  125/126/127 could-not-run, 128+n killed by signal
#                  n, e.g. 137 = OOM kill).  Container events.  MUST
#                  NOT be read as open fibers or repaired by editing
#                  modules.
#   aggregate      raw exit code of `agda NaturalMachine.agda`, or `-`
#                  when it was not invoked (toolchain=absent).
#   walk_frontier  last `next n ≡ m` in WalkBridge.agda, or `-`.
#
# Migration note: on 2026-08-16 the five pre-schema rows were rewritten
# in place into these columns (the one permitted breach of append-only,
# recorded here).  Cycles 0-3: toolchain unrecorded -> `-`; their 0
# fibers split as 0/0.  Cycle 4: its 315 nonzero exits reclassified
# from `fiber` to `fiber_env` on the evidence above (agda absent =>
# every exit was 127).  No counts were altered, only classified.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT IT DOES NOT DO
#
# It does not invent mathematics and does not call a model.  It keeps the
# substrate live — published, gated, ledgered, and freshly seeded — so
# that any mind attaching to it, at any hour, starts from a true picture
# and an unbiased door.  The mathematics is in `formal/cubical/`; this
# file is the thing that makes it a machine rather than a directory.
# =====================================================================

set -u
cd "$(dirname "$0")"

MODE="${1:-once}"
INTERVAL="${2:-600}"

CUBICAL=formal/cubical
LEDGER=collab/orchestration/machine-ledger.tsv
FIBERS=collab/orchestration/open-fibers.md
STAMP() { date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ; }

# GNU `timeout` is not present on the macOS host used by this workspace.  A
# missing watchdog must not turn every mathematical check into exit 127.
# Prefer a real watchdog where available; otherwise run the check directly and
# preserve its actual exit code.  The outer supervisor still keeps the loop
# alive, while the ledger records genuine Agda results instead of command
# lookup failures.
run_checked() {
    if command -v timeout >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        timeout 900 "$@"
    elif command -v gtimeout >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        gtimeout 900 "$@"
    else
        "$@"
    fi
}

mkdir -p collab/orchestration
[ -f "$LEDGER" ] || printf 'utc\tcycle\ttoolchain\tmodules\tgreen\tfiber\tfiber_env\taggregate\twalk_frontier\n' > "$LEDGER"

cycle_number() { [ -f "$LEDGER" ] && echo $(( $(wc -l < "$LEDGER") - 1 )) || echo 0; }

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. SYNC
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
phase_sync() { [ -x ./sync ] && ./sync >/dev/null 2>&1; return 0; }

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. GATE — per module, exit code recorded, never inferred from output
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
phase_gate() {
    GREEN=0; FIBER=0; FIBER_ENV=0; FIBER_LIST=""; ENV_LIST=""

    # The toolchain is part of the observation, so it is captured first
    # and written into the row.  Without it, a container event (agda
    # absent, cycle 4) is indistinguishable from a mathematical state
    # (corpus dead), which is exactly the confusion the 2026-08-14T17:19Z
    # row caused.
    TOOLCHAIN=$(agda --version 2>/dev/null | head -1 | awk '{print "agda-" $3}')
    if [ -z "$TOOLCHAIN" ]; then
        TOOLCHAIN="absent"
        AGG="-"
        return 0     # 0 checks performed, not N failed ones.
    fi

    while IFS= read -r f; do
        rel="${f#"$CUBICAL"/}"
        ( cd "$CUBICAL" && LC_ALL=C.UTF-8 run_checked agda -i . "$rel" ) >/dev/null 2>&1
        RC=$?
        if [ "$RC" -eq 0 ]; then
            GREEN=$((GREEN+1))
        elif [ "$RC" -ge 124 ]; then
            # 124 watchdog timeout, 125/126/127 could-not-run, 128+n
            # signal death: the environment's exit, not Agda's verdict.
            FIBER_ENV=$((FIBER_ENV+1))
            ENV_LIST="${ENV_LIST}${rel} (exit ${RC})"$'\n'
        else
            FIBER=$((FIBER+1))
            FIBER_LIST="${FIBER_LIST}${rel}"$'\n'
        fi
    done < <(find "$CUBICAL" -name '*.agda' -not -path '*/_build/*' -not -path '*/Control/*' | sort)

    # `Control/` is excluded BECAUSE its contents must fail to typecheck
    # (NaturalMachine.agda's own header says so).  Counting deliberate
    # refutations as fibers would be a false red, which is the same
    # failure as a false green with the sign flipped.

    ( cd "$CUBICAL" && LC_ALL=C.UTF-8 run_checked agda -i . NaturalMachine.agda ) >/dev/null 2>&1
    AGG=$?
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. LEDGER — append-only; and the open fibers written where they are read
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
phase_ledger() {
    # A due-by stamp makes death self-reporting: no process is needed in
    # order to detect that no process is running.
    DUE_BY=$(date -u -d "+$(( INTERVAL + 300 )) seconds" +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ 2>/dev/null \
             || echo "unknown")
    N=$(cycle_number)
    TOTAL=$((GREEN + FIBER + FIBER_ENV))
    printf '%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\n' \
        "$(STAMP)" "$N" "$TOOLCHAIN" "$TOTAL" "$GREEN" "$FIBER" "$FIBER_ENV" \
        "$AGG" "$WALK" >> "$LEDGER"

    {
        echo "# Open fibers — regenerated every cycle, do not hand-edit"
        echo
        echo "Cycle $N, $(STAMP). Toolchain: \`$TOOLCHAIN\` (pin: Agda 2.8.0 +"
        echo "cubical v0.9, \`formal/cubical/BUILD.md\`). Aggregate"
        echo "\`NaturalMachine.agda\` exit: **$AGG**."
        echo "Modules invoked individually: $TOTAL. Green: $GREEN."
        echo "Mathematical fibers: $FIBER. Environment events: $FIBER_ENV."
        echo
        echo "**Next cycle due by $DUE_BY.** If you are reading this after that"
        echo "time, the machine is not running — restart it with one cycle:"
        echo '`./run_the_natural_machine_forever`. A dead loop and a quiet loop'
        echo "are indistinguishable from the repository unless the loop says when"
        echo "it will be back, which is why this line exists (msg 0463: a sibling"
        echo "machine was dead an hour before anyone noticed)."
        echo
        if [ "$TOOLCHAIN" = "absent" ]; then
            echo "**No agda on PATH: this cycle checked nothing.** The row it"
            echo "wrote is a statement about this container, not about the"
            echo "mathematics — zero modules were invoked, so the corpus's last"
            echo "known state is the previous row written by a container that"
            echo "had a toolchain."
        elif [ "$TOOLCHAIN" != "agda-2.8.0" ]; then
            echo "**Toolchain differs from the pin.** Fibers listed below may be"
            echo "version skew (BUILD.md \"Where the skew bites\"), which is"
            echo "evidence about the container, not the tree. Align the"
            echo "container with the pin before treating any fiber below as a"
            echo "reconstruction question; do not edit modules to appease the"
            echo "wrong toolchain."
            echo
        fi
        if [ "$TOOLCHAIN" != "absent" ]; then
            if [ "$FIBER" -eq 0 ]; then
                echo "No open fibers. Every module under \`formal/cubical/\` that"
                echo "this toolchain can judge returns exit 0 on its own, not"
                echo "merely as part of an aggregate."
            else
                echo "Delta 15 C15.82: each of these is a reconstruction question,"
                echo "not an error. The module named is the one whose fiber is"
                echo "noncontractible or empty; that fiber is the assignment."
                echo
                printf '%s' "$FIBER_LIST" | sed '/^$/d' | sed 's/^/- `/;s/$/`/'
            fi
            if [ "$FIBER_ENV" -gt 0 ]; then
                echo
                echo "Environment events (NOT fibers — the container failed, the"
                echo "module was never judged; exit 124 = watchdog timeout,"
                echo "125-127 = could not run, 128+n = killed by signal n):"
                echo
                printf '%s' "$ENV_LIST" | sed '/^$/d' | sed 's/^/- `/;s/$/`/'
            fi
        fi
    } > "$FIBERS"
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#    the walk's own frontier, read from what is CHECKED, not recomputed
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
phase_walk() {
    WALK=$(grep -ho 'next [0-9]* ≡ [0-9]*' \
            "$CUBICAL"/NaturalMachine/WalkBridge.agda 2>/dev/null \
           | tail -1 | tr -d ' ' )
    [ -n "$WALK" ] || WALK="-"
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. DRAW — a fresh uncurated door, so the next arrival does not enter
#    through the conspicuous documents
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
phase_draw() {
    S=random_entry_seeder_so_agents_dont_cluster
    [ -x "$S/seed.sh" ] || return 0
    mkdir -p collab/orchestration/draws
    "$S/seed.sh" "machine-cycle-$(cycle_number)" \
        > "collab/orchestration/draws/next-door.txt" 2>/dev/null
    return 0
}

run_cycle() {
    phase_sync
    phase_walk
    phase_gate
    phase_ledger
    phase_draw
    phase_sync
    echo "MACHINE cycle $(( $(cycle_number) - 1 )) [${TOOLCHAIN}]: ${GREEN} green, ${FIBER} fibers, ${FIBER_ENV} env events, aggregate ${AGG}, walk ${WALK} — $(STAMP)"
}

case "$MODE" in
    --supervise)
        # For environments where processes DO persist.  A cycle that
        # returns nonzero never stops the loop — T15.81 again: there is no
        # failure branch, only a fiber.  Note this does not save you from
        # the supervisor itself being reaped, which is exactly what
        # happened in this container; the due-by stamp covers that case.
        echo "MACHINE: supervised loop started, every ${INTERVAL}s (pid $$)"
        while :; do
            ( run_cycle ) || echo "MACHINE: cycle nonzero; continuing"
            sleep "$INTERVAL"
        done
        ;;
    --daemon)
        echo "MACHINE: hands-free loop started, every ${INTERVAL}s (pid $$)"
        while :; do run_cycle; sleep "$INTERVAL"; done
        ;;
    *)
        run_cycle
        ;;
esac
